🧰 Core Java Command-Line Tools

These tools (javac, javap, jar, javadoc, jdb, …) ship with the:
✅ JDK (Java Development Kit)

Quick split:
  JDK  → development tools (javac, jar, javadoc, jdb, javap, …) + JRE
  JRE  → runtime only → mainly `java` (run apps)
  JVM  → inside JRE → actually executes bytecode


1. 🛠️ javac — Java Compiler
Usage: Compiles .java → .class (bytecode)

  javac HelloWorld.java          → HelloWorld.class
  javac -d out HelloWorld.java   → put .class into out/ folder
  javac -cp lib/* App.java       → compile with classpath

Interview: javac does NOT run the program — only compiles.


2. 🔎 javap — Java Class File Disassembler
Usage: Shows what’s inside a .class (signatures / bytecode)

Why “disassembler”?
  .java  --javac-->  bytecode (.class)
  javap  --reads-->  human-readable form of that bytecode

  javap HelloWorld          → public methods / fields
  javap -c HelloWorld       → bytecode instructions (iload, invokevirtual…)
  javap -v HelloWorld       → verbose (constant pool, etc.)

⚡ Disassembler ≠ Decompiler
  Disassembler (javap) → bytecode mnemonics
  Decompiler (JD-GUI…) → approximate .java source

Use: debug compiled code, verify compiler output, peek libraries


3. 🧪 java — Java Runtime Launcher
Usage: Runs compiled .class or a JAR (starts the JVM)

  java HelloWorld                 → run class (needs HelloWorld.class on classpath)
  java -cp out HelloWorld         → class from out/
  java -jar app.jar               → run executable JAR (needs Main-Class in manifest)
  java -Xmx512m HelloWorld        → set max heap

Interview: `java` is in JRE/JDK; `javac` only in JDK.


4. 📦 jar — Java Archive Tool
Usage: Create / list / extract .jar (zip of .class + resources + META-INF)

  jar cf  app.jar *.class         → c=create, f=file
  jar tf  app.jar                 → t=list (table), f=file
  jar xf  app.jar                 → x=extract
  jar cfe app.jar MainClass *.class → e=entry/Main-Class in manifest

JAR ≈ ZIP with Java metadata (manifest).


5. 🧬 javadoc — Documentation Generator
Usage: HTML docs from /** Javadoc */ comments

  javadoc MyClass.java
  javadoc -d docs src/*.java      → output folder docs/


6. 🕵️‍♂️ jdb — Java Debugger
Usage: Command-line debugger (breakpoints, step, print vars)

  jdb HelloWorld

(Most people use IDE debugger; jdb = CLI equivalent.)


7. 📈 Monitoring / profiling tools

| Tool        | Purpose                                      | Typical “when”              |
| ----------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| `jps`       | List running Java process IDs                | Find PID first              |
| `jconsole`  | GUI monitor (heap, threads, CPU)             | Live health check           |
| `jvisualvm` | Profiling, heap dump analysis, sampling      | Find CPU/memory hotspots    |
| `jstack`    | Thread dump (what each thread is doing)      | Deadlock / hung app         |
| `jmap`      | Heap summary / heap dump                     | Memory leak investigation   |
| `jstat`     | GC & class-loading stats over time           | GC tuning / watch GC pauses |

Common flow for hung app:
  jps  →  get PID
  jstack <pid>  →  thread dump (look for deadlock)


🧠 Interview one-liner
“JDK tools: javac compiles, java runs, jar packages, javap disassembles bytecode, javadoc docs, jdb debugs; jstack/jmap/jstat/jconsole diagnose live JVMs.”

Quick recall:
  Source → javac → Bytecode → java (JVM) → Run
  Peek bytecode → javap -c
  Package → jar
  Hang/deadlock → jstack
  Memory → jmap / jstat
